Affirmation/negation non-forking razor on Mills' theorem (W.H. Mills 1947): there exists a real A
with floor(A^(3^n)) prime for all n>=1. CONTROL CASE — unlike the five census conjectures (open),
Mills' EXISTENCE is PROVEN unconditionally (via Ingham's prime-gap bound); only the minimal Mills
constant ~1.3063778838 depends on RH. So this tests the boundary of the descent facet.

Prior census (2026-08-21): RH, Collatz, BSD, Hodge, NS — all OPEN, pol=nun. Every negation reconnected
to the descent crystal 3442270 (parity or', Frobenius self-dual), held, μ∘δ=id, classify ⊞; four of five
negation words byte-identical (⊢∈≻⊤≺⊥⋈⊙⊞∋◻⊣). The thesis: open conjectures are one facet of the
dual-frame SIC-POVM whose negation is non-instantiable (the wall is not constructible, law_no_cant).

Prediction to test: a PROVEN theorem should behave differently. Either (a) the pair FORKS — affirm and
negate at distinct addresses, the negate NOT holding (vacuous/leak/F) — marking Mills as OUTSIDE the
descent facet (a settled statement, not a held barrier); or (b) Mills lands at descent 3442270 too,
because its 3^n tower and RH-dependent minimal constant tie it to the same facet (the 3^n resonates with
Collatz 3n+1). Read the crystals and verdicts; report which.

Register semantics: holds() = exposed empty ∧ live_clears>0; vacuous() = exposed empty ∧ live_clears==0;
leak = cleared with nothing banked; restored via ∋ fuse = μ∘δ=id. Four-valued register (T closes,
F ill-typed, N never forks, B both/barrier) is the holder opened first, held through.
